204:<li class="ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_paragraph"><a href="#S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" title="In 4 Conclusion ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title">Acknowledgments</span></a></li>
206:<li class="ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_bibliography"><a href="#bib" title="In Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title">References</span></a></li>
218:<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two</h1>
230:<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">The nonnegative integer rank of a matrix is a variant of the classical nonnegative rank, introduced in the 1980s, where factorizations are required to have integer entries. While computing nonnegative integer rank is generally very hard, we focus on a fundamental special case: determining when a rank 2 nonnegative integer matrix has nonnegative integer rank equal to 2 (the “rank2 problem”). Although this problem is trivial in the continuous case, in this context it is surprisingly rich.</p>
232:<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p">We provide a geometric reformulation in terms of affine semigroups and rational cones in the plane, which yields new structural insights. We show that any rank 2 integer matrix can be reduced to a <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 3</annotation></semantics></math> matrix which has nonnegative integer rank <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>
233:if and only if the original one also has nonnegative integer rank <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, with the reduction computable in polynomial time. This reduction reveals that the difficulty of the rank2 problem is already captured by small matrices.
234:Building on this geometric framework, we also develop an algorithm that solves the rank2 problem by strategically searching for integer generators within bounded regions of the associated cone. Although the theoretical worst-case complexity remains high, numerical experiments demonstrate that the algorithm performs efficiently in practice.</p>
241:<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The nonnegative rank of a matrix, <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rankp(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>rank^{+}</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rankp(A)</annotation></semantics></math>, has been studied since the 1970s (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>) and has developed a large body of literature in recent years due to its connection with extension complexities of polytopes and nonnegative matrix factorizations. A modern presentation of the topic and its applications can be found in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.
242:In this paper, we will study a lesser known integer variant of this quantity, the <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">nonnegative integer rank</em> of a matrix <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rankzn(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>rank</mi><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rankzn(A)</annotation></semantics></math>, which was introduced in the 1980s (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>).</p>
245:<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Both these ranks are special cases of what is known as a semiring or a factorization rank (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>). Given <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}^{n\times m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}^{n\times m}</annotation></semantics></math>, for any semiring <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> that contains <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> one can define <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rank_{S}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>rank</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rank_{S}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> as the smallest <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> for which there exist <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in S^{n\times k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in S^{n\times k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\in S^{k\times m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\in S^{k\times m}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=BC" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=BC</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
248:<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">nonnegative integer rank</em> of matrix <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rankzn(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>rank</mi><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rankzn(A)</annotation></semantics></math>, is the special case when <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\mathbb{Z}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\mathbb{Z}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\mathbb{R}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\mathbb{R}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> we get the usual nonnegative rank <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rankp(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>rank^{+}</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rankp(A)</annotation></semantics></math>, and for <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> we simply obtain the rank. In particular we have
254:<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rank(A)\leq\rankp(A)\leq\rankzn(A)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>rank^{+}</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>rank</mi><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rank(A)\leq\rankp(A)\leq\rankzn(A).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
258:<p id="S1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> for a more general discussion of these ranks.</p>
261:<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Although much is known about <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rankp(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>rank^{+}</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rankp(A)</annotation></semantics></math>, very few results exist for <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rankzn(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>rank</mi><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rankzn(A)</annotation></semantics></math>. For the special case where <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix, the nonnegative integer rank can be seen as the bipartite clique partition number of a bipartite graph. Under this guise it has been extensively studied, both in the computer science and in the linear algebra community, since the groundbreaking work of Orlin <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> which proposed its NP-completeness as an open problem (later confirmed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>). In the more general framework, however, most of the research focuses on identifying classes of matrices for which certain ranks coincide (for example, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>). There do not seem to exist lower bounds that are not directly obtained through (<a href="#S1.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).</p>
264:<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, the authors of this paper have shown that, similarly to the nonnegative rank, the nonnegative integer rank can be interpreted in terms of certain extension complexities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. Additionally, in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, Dong et al study the closely related integer nonnegative matrix approximation problem, providing a specialized algorithm for it and making a good case that there are application advantages of working over the integers directly, while providing good applied examples where the nonnegative integer rank admit natural interpretations. These connections motivate us to develop the theoretical foundations of this notion, a program we set in motion in the present paper.</p>
267:<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Computing the nonnegative integer rank of a matrix is a very hard problem, so we will begin to look into simpler versions of that problem. Given the basic inequalities presented in (<a href="#S1.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), a natural question is the following.</p>
270:<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(The minrank problem)<span id="S1.Thmthm1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
272:<p id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a nonnegative matrix <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> how do we check if <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rankzn(A)=\rank(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>rank</mi><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rankzn(A)=\rank(A)</annotation></semantics></math>?</p>
276:<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">This question is still very hard. The corresponding question for the nonnegative rank (called ExactNMF) is known to be NP-hard <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, but when restricted to matrices of fixed rank <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,2</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>, it becomes solvable in polynomial time <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. In the case of rank one, the minrank problem is trivial, since every nonnegative integer matrix with rank one can easily be seen to have nonnegative integer rank one. However, contrary to what happens with the usual nonnegative rank, the integer rank two case is not trivial.</p>
279:<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">For nonnegative matrices, having rank two implies having nonnegative rank two, but it is not true that it implies having nonnegative integer rank two. A known example of this is the matrix</p>
291:<p id="S1.p8.2" class="ltx_p">from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, Example 4.2]</cite>, which has rank (and consequently nonnegative rank) two but nonnegative integer rank three. Moreover, in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> it is shown that the nonnegative integer rank can in fact be arbitrarily high for rank two matrices. This means the following restricted version of the minrank problem is already of significant interest.</p>
294:<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(The rank<math id="S1.Thmthm2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> problem)<span id="S1.Thmthm2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
296:<p id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a nonnegative matrix <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> how do we check if <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rankzn(A)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>rank</mi><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rankzn(A)=2</annotation></semantics></math>?</p>
300:<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">On a different but related direction we have the result of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> where it shown for <math id="S1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 2</annotation></semantics></math> integer completely positive matrices, that they must have an integer cp-factorization.</p>
303:<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we present an answer to <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Problem 1.2 (The rank2 problem). ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Problem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> in the form of an algorithm that is inspired by a geometric reformulation of the problem.
304:The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. In <a href="#S2" title="2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we reinterpret the nonnegative integer rank of a rank 2 matrix as a geometric question about finding integer generators in a rational cone in <math id="S1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. We introduce a canonical diagram representation and show that for any rank 2 nonnegative integer matrix we can reduce it to a <math id="S1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 3</annotation></semantics></math> matrix which has nonnegative integer rank 2 if and only if the original one also has nonnegative integer rank 2, with the reduction procedure being computationally efficient. In <a href="#S3" title="3 Algorithm ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we present our algorithm for solving the rank2 problem, which exploits the geometric structure by decomposing the planar cone introduced in <a href="#S2" title="2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and searching over a bounded region of candidate generators. We provide detailed numerical tests demonstrating that despite the theoretical hardness of the problem, our algorithm runs in reasonable time even for large matrices.</p>
308:<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>A geometric view of the rank2 problem</h2>
314:<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We start by noting that for the minrank problem, Problem <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Problem 1.1 (The minrank problem). ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>, if we have an <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times m</annotation></semantics></math> nonnegative integer matrix <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> of rank <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, a positive answer corresponds to the existence of nonnegative integer matrices <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> of sizes <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\times k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\times k</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, such that <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=BC^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=BC^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>. This would imply that the column space of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> equals the column space of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, since every column of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is a combination of the columns of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> and the dimension of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\col(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>col</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\col(B)</annotation></semantics></math> is at most <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
317:<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">If we consider the affine semigroup <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma=\col(A)\cap\mathbb{Z}^{n}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>col</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma=\col(A)\cap\mathbb{Z}^{n}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> of dimension <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, then the minrank problem can be framed as asking if there exist <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> elements in <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> that span a semigroup that contains all columns of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>. This leads to the following equivalent version of the minrank problem.</p>
326:<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Although this interpretation holds for every rank <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, in the case of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math> the geometry is particularly simple. Given a nonnegative matrix <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{Z}^{n\times m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{Z}^{n\times m}</annotation></semantics></math> of rank 2, we define the cone <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{A}=\col(A)\cap\mathbb{R}_{+}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>col</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{A}=\col(A)\cap\mathbb{R}_{+}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> has rank 2, <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\col(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>col</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\col(A)</annotation></semantics></math> is a 2-dimensional subspace, and hence we can find a transformation that maps the lattice <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\col(A)\cap\mathbb{Z}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>col</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\col(A)\cap\mathbb{Z}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> bijectively to <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. We can therefore think of the rank<math id="S2.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> problem in the following way.</p>
337:<p id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider again the rank <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> matrix</p>
351:<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.05957v2/NNI_rank2_ex23.png" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:215/180;" width="215" height="180" alt="Refer to caption">
352:<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Graphical interpretation of Example <a href="#S2.Thmthm3" title="Example 2.3. ‣ 2.1 A geometric reformulation ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>.</figcaption>
355:<p id="S2.Thmthm3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Example 2.3. ‣ 2.1 A geometric reformulation ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> we can see the graphical interpretation. We are asking if there are two lattice points in the blue region that generate all three red points. One can easily see that both <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,0)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math> have to be there since no other points generate them, in other words, they are part of the Hilbert basis of the affine semigroup obtained by intersecting the cone with the integer lattice. Since they do not generate <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4,3)</annotation></semantics></math> this immediately tells us the nonnegative integer rank of <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is indeed <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
361:<p id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider now the family of rank <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> matrices</p>
373:<p id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">for any integer <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. The lattice <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\col(B)\cap\mathbb{Z}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>col</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\col(B)\cap\mathbb{Z}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by the vectors <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1,1)^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1,1)^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,0,-1)^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,0,-1)^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying the linear transformation that sends these points to <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,0)</annotation></semantics></math>, we get that the columns of <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are sent to <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1,t)</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,t)</annotation></semantics></math>, and the cone <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\col(B_{t})\cap\mathbb{R}_{+}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>col</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\col(B_{t})\cap\mathbb{R}_{+}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> is sent to the cone <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> spanned by <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. We can see the diagram for <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=4</annotation></semantics></math> in Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Example 2.4. ‣ 2.1 A geometric reformulation ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. For other values of <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the diagram will be the same but with the red points at height <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
375:<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.05957v2/NNI_rank2_ex24.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/134;" width="238" height="134" alt="Refer to caption">
376:<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Graphical interpretation of Example <a href="#S2.Thmthm4" title="Example 2.4. ‣ 2.1 A geometric reformulation ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>.</figcaption>
379:<p id="S2.Thmthm4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This case is a bit less obvious, as there are many possible generators to check (in theory, every point in the blue cone of height at most <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>). However, we will show that no choice of pairs of points can generate the three red points; thus, this family of matrices always has a nonnegative integer rank <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
394:<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the graphical diagram associated to a particular rank2 problem is not unique. This is because we can compose the isomorphism from <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\col(A)\cap\mathbb{Z}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>col</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\col(A)\cap\mathbb{Z}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, which we use to generate the diagram, with any linear automorphism of <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. For consistency, we can define a canonical diagram corresponding to a matrix as follows.</p>
424:<p id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Problem <a href="#S2.Thmthm2" title="Problem 2.2. ‣ 2.1 A geometric reformulation ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> we can always assume <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and is generated by <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,0)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c,d)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq c&lt;d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq c&lt;d</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
430:<p id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let us consider the example in Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Example 2.4. ‣ 2.1 A geometric reformulation ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, coming from the case of Example <a href="#S2.Thmthm4" title="Example 2.4. ‣ 2.1 A geometric reformulation ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a> where <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=4</annotation></semantics></math> so that we are considering the matrix</p>
445:<p id="S2.Thmthm6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The composition of the two maps gives us the transformation <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)\mapsto(y,y-x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)\mapsto(y,y-x)</annotation></semantics></math> and when we apply it to the full diagram we get the canonical form shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Example 2.6. ‣ 2.1 A geometric reformulation ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
447:<figure id="S2.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.05957v2/NNI_rank2_ex26.png" id="S2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:215/249;" width="215" height="249" alt="Refer to caption">
448:<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Transformation of Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Example 2.4. ‣ 2.1 A geometric reformulation ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> to a canonical form.</figcaption>
452:<p id="S2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">A source of ambiguity remains in this canonical form, since we can pick either of the two minimal generators of the cone to map to <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,0)</annotation></semantics></math>, resulting in two different diagrams. Another thing to point out is that while the existence of such a form will be very useful to streamline arguments, there may be occasions where a different form of the diagram is more suitable. Example <a href="#S2.Thmthm4" title="Example 2.4. ‣ 2.1 A geometric reformulation ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a> is a case where the canonical embedding is less amenable to work with than the initial embedding we used.</p>
459:<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We saw in Section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 A geometric reformulation ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> how to go from the rank2 problem to the problem of finding two generators in a rational cone for a finite set of points. We will start this section by trying the reverse: given a rational cone <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and a set of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> integer points <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1},...,a_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1},...,a_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> in it, we want to construct a matrix that has nonnegative integer rank <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if there are two integer points in <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> that nonnegatively span <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1},...,a_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1},...,a_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
467:<p id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We will start by considering the example in Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Example 2.3. ‣ 2.1 A geometric reformulation ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Our goal is to try to recover a matrix simply from the figure data. A first attempt would be to take the integer inequalities defining the faces of the cone <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
485:<p id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.3" class="ltx_p">However, this matrix has obviously nonnegative integer rank <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, since the columns are generated by <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,0)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. The issue is that <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,0)</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the image of a non-integer point from <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, the point <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1/3,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1/3,0)</annotation></semantics></math>. We would like the integer points in the column space of our matrix to be images of points in <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
506:<p id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.5" class="ltx_p">In this case, we can see that we recovered precisely the original matrix from Example <a href="#S2.Thmthm3" title="Example 2.3. ‣ 2.1 A geometric reformulation ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>, only with permuted rows.</p>
510:<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.8</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
512:<p id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a rational pointed cone with nonempty interior <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> integer points <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1},...,a_{m}\in K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1},...,a_{m}\in K</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times m</annotation></semantics></math> matrix <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rankzn(B)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>rank</mi><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rankzn(B)=2</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if there are two integer points in <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> such that every <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a nonnegative combination of them.</span></p>
518:<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">To simplify the argument, note that by <a href="#S2.Thmthm5" title="Observation 2.5. ‣ 2.1 A geometric reformulation ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Observation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a> we can assume that <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in the nonnegative quadrant and one of its rays is generated by <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,0)</annotation></semantics></math>.
527:<p id="S2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We already knew how to go from the rank<math id="S2.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> problem for an <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times m</annotation></semantics></math> matrix <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> to Problem <a href="#S2.Thmthm2" title="Problem 2.2. ‣ 2.1 A geometric reformulation ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>, with <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> integer points. Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm8" title="Proposition 2.8. ‣ 2.2 Reduction to the 3×3 case ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a> now tells us that we can go from that problem to a the rank<math id="S2.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> problem on a <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times m</annotation></semantics></math> matrix. We want to interpret this reduction directly in terms of matrices, bypassing the geometric construction. The general underlying idea is the following.</p>
530:<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.9</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
532:<p id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> be rank <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> nonnegative integer matrices of size <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times m</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\times m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\times m</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. If <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy the following conditions:</span></p>
547:<p id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">then either <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rankzn(A)=\rankzn(B)=r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>rank</mi><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>rank</mi><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rankzn(A)=\rankzn(B)=r</annotation></semantics></math> or they are both greater than <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
559:<p id="S2.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now note that <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rankzn(A)=r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>rank</mi><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rankzn(A)=r</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=MN" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=MN</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> are nonnegative integer matrices and <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times r</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that the columns of <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> are in the column space of <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, hence we can apply <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> to each of them to get a new matrix <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\prime}\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}^{k\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\prime}\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}^{k\times r}</annotation></semantics></math>. The linearity of <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> then immediately tells us that <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\prime}N=B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\prime}N=B</annotation></semantics></math> which tells us that <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rankzn(B)=r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>rank</mi><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rankzn(B)=r</annotation></semantics></math>. The same argument gives us the reverse implication.
564:<p id="S2.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The argument in Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm8" title="Proposition 2.8. ‣ 2.2 Reduction to the 3×3 case ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a> boils down to saying that for every <math id="S2.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times m</annotation></semantics></math> nonnegative integer matrix <math id="S2.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> we can construct a <math id="S2.SS2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times m</annotation></semantics></math> nonnegative integer matrix <math id="S2.SS2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the conditions of Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm9" title="Lemma 2.9. ‣ 2.2 Reduction to the 3×3 case ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.9</span></a>. In what follows we show how to construct such a matrix explicitly.</p>
573:<p id="S2.SS2.p12.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure this is not the case, we want to find an integer element <math id="S2.SS2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{\row}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>row</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{\row}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>, obtained as a positive rational combination of <math id="S2.SS2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, that together with <math id="S2.SS2.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> spans that lattice, and add it to <math id="S2.SS2.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. This is always possible, since any primitive element of a rank 2 lattice can be extended to a basis of said lattice by adding some element. By adding multiples of <math id="S2.SS2.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> to this new element, we can then guarantee that it becomes a positive combination of <math id="S2.SS2.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, without changing the lattice spanned.</p>
576:<p id="S2.SS2.p13.1" class="ltx_p">To do this in practice, find a basis <math id="S2.SS2.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1},a_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1},a_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for the lattice <math id="S2.SS2.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{\row}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>row</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{\row}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>, by using the LLL algorithm for instance, and write <math id="S2.SS2.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in terms of that basis, obtaining <math id="S2.SS2.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math>. The primitivity of <math id="S2.SS2.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> implies <math id="S2.SS2.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{gcd}(p,q)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>gcd</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{gcd}(p,q)=1</annotation></semantics></math>, so by using the extended euclidean algorithm, we can find <math id="S2.SS2.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r,s\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r,s\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS2.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="rp-sq=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">rp-sq=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.SS2.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{b_{3}}=sa_{1}+ra_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{b_{3}}=sa_{1}+ra_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> spans the lattice <math id="S2.SS2.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{\row}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>row</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{\row}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> together with <math id="S2.SS2.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. By adding positive multiples of <math id="S2.SS2.p13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> we can replace <math id="S2.SS2.p13.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{b_{3}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{b_{3}}</annotation></semantics></math> by a <math id="S2.SS2.p13.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> that is a positive combination of <math id="S2.SS2.p13.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p13.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="S2.SS2.p13.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{b_{1},b_{2},b_{3}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{b_{1},b_{2},b_{3}\}</annotation></semantics></math> span the lattice as intended. It is now simple to check that this matrix <math id="S2.SS2.p13.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies all the conditions of <a href="#S2.Thmthm9" title="Lemma 2.9. ‣ 2.2 Reduction to the 3×3 case ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.9</span></a>.</p>
579:<p id="S2.SS2.p14.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the construction of <math id="S2.SS2.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is computationally simple. The only nontrivial steps of the procedure are the use of LLL and the extended euclidean algorithm, both of which are polynomial in the bitlengths of the numbers, and on the sizes of the matrices involved.</p>
582:<p id="S2.SS2.p15.1" class="ltx_p">We can now leverage this construction, applying it twice. If we have an <math id="S2.SS2.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times m</annotation></semantics></math> matrix of rank <math id="S2.SS2.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> fow which we want to solve the minrank problem, we can apply Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm8" title="Proposition 2.8. ‣ 2.2 Reduction to the 3×3 case ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a> to obtain a <math id="S2.SS2.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times n</annotation></semantics></math> matrix with the same solution. Now, since transposing a matrix does not change its ranks, we can apply the proposition again to the transpose of this matrix, attaining a <math id="S2.SS2.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 3</annotation></semantics></math> matrix that will also have the same solution as the original one. We thus obtain the following fact.</p>
585:<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.10</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
587:<p id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given a rank <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> nonnegative integer matrix <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}^{n\times m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}^{n\times m}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can always construct a <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 3</annotation></semantics></math> nonnegative integer matrix <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> of rank <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> whose nonnegative integer rank is <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if the nonnegative integer rank of <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, the computational effort and the norms of the entries of <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> are polynomial in <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n,m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n,m</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log(\max_{i,j}|A_{ij}|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log(\max_{i,j}|A_{ij}|)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
593:<p id="S2.Thmthm11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One could think that a similar geometric reduction would work for other minrank problems, not necessarily just rank <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>. However, this construction makes key use of the fact that the cone <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\col(A)\cap\mathbb{R}_{+}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>col</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\col(A)\cap\mathbb{R}_{+}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is two dimensional, and any two dimensional cone is defined by at most two inequalities. For any other rank, the number of inequalities defining <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\col(A)\cap\mathbb{R}_{+}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>col</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\col(A)\cap\mathbb{R}_{+}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> can be <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, which makes this geometric approach useless.</p>
600:Consider the rank <math id="S2.Thmthm12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> matrix</p>
618:<p id="S2.Thmthm12.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Its diagram is the first in Figure <a href="#S2.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Example 2.12. ‣ 2.2 Reduction to the 3×3 case ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
622:<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2602.05957v2/NNI_rank2_ex211-1.png" id="S2.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:167/230;" width="167" height="230" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2602.05957v2/NNI_rank2_ex211-2.png" id="S2.F4.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:167/230;" width="167" height="230" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
655:<p id="S2.Thmthm12.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In terms of the basis in (<a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ Example 2.12. ‣ 2.2 Reduction to the 3×3 case ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) before these rows correspond to vectors <math id="S2.Thmthm12.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(5,-3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(5,-3)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm12.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,-2)</annotation></semantics></math>. Taking the first vector as <math id="S2.Thmthm12.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> we have that <math id="S2.Thmthm12.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times(-1)-(-3)\times 2=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times(-1)-(-3)\times 2=1</annotation></semantics></math>, hence we get <math id="S2.Thmthm12.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{b_{3}}=(2,-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{b_{3}}=(2,-1)</annotation></semantics></math> in terms of our basis, which corresponds to <math id="S2.Thmthm12.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,3,4,1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,3,4,1,0)</annotation></semantics></math>. In terms of <math id="S2.Thmthm12.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm12.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> this is <math id="S2.Thmthm12.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{7}(3b_{1}-b_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>7</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{7}(3b_{1}-b_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> so we
669:<p id="S2.Thmthm12.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Notice that these three rows are very much related to the original matrix: they are the third, fourth and first rows of <math id="S2.Thmthm12.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> up to multipliers. The diagram of (the transpose) of this matrix is the second one in Figure <a href="#S2.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Example 2.12. ‣ 2.2 Reduction to the 3×3 case ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
703:<p id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The fact that we can reduce checking if nonnegative integer rank equals 2 to the case of <math id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 3</annotation></semantics></math> matrices could suggest that, in analogy to the usual rank, if every <math id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 3</annotation></semantics></math> submatrix has nonnegative integer rank <math id="S2.Thmthm13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> than so does have the full matrix. However, as the following matrix shows, this is wrong. In Figure <a href="#S2.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Example 2.13. ‣ 2.2 Reduction to the 3×3 case ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> we can see the diagram of the matrix</p>
717:<figure id="S2.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.05957v2/NNI_rank2_ex212.png" id="S2.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:143/237;" width="143" height="237" alt="Refer to caption">
730:<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the previous section we saw that checking if an <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times m</annotation></semantics></math> matrix of rank <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> also has nonnegative integer rank <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> can be reduced to checking it for a <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 3</annotation></semantics></math> matrix. Unfortunately, this does not get us any closer to solving this problem, it just tells us that the <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 3</annotation></semantics></math> version of the problem is as hard as the original one. In this section, we present an algorithm that actually solves the rank2 problem.</p>
733:<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A first approach to the problem would be a brute force search: given a matrix <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, we are looking for two integer points in the 2-dimensional cone of its diagram that generate all the points corresponding to columns of <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> as nonnegative combinations (see Problem <a href="#S2.Thmthm2" title="Problem 2.2. ‣ 2.1 A geometric reformulation ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> and the discussion around it). Since each generator has to be in the nonnegative orthant and be entrywise smaller than or equal to those points, we have a finite search space that we could exhaust.</p>
736:<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This approach certainly works, but is impractical due to the size of the search space. The main idea of our proposed algorithm is to use the geometric interpretation of <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 A geometric reformulation ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> to restrict the search space for nonnegative integer vectors that factorize the given matrix (see Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Acknowledgments ‣ 4 Conclusion ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). The intuition for reducing the search space can be seen in Example <a href="#S2.Thmthm4" title="Example 2.4. ‣ 2.1 A geometric reformulation ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>, where we note that in order to generate the points of <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\col(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>col</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\col(A)</annotation></semantics></math> nonnegatively, our 2 generators must somehow live “outside” this set of points. We formalize this idea below.</p>
742:<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The algorithm receives a rank <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> nonnegative matrix <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, of size <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times m</annotation></semantics></math> and proceeds according to the following steps.</p>
749:<math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\col(A)\cap\mathbb{Z}_{+}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>col</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\col(A)\cap\mathbb{Z}_{+}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and map it to <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. This can be done by computing the Smith Normal form of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>). After that we can perform a unimodular transformation to obtain a canonical form as discussed in <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 A geometric reformulation ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>.</p>
768:<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">as pictured in Figure <a href="#S3.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 3.1.2 Decomposing the Cone ‣ 3.1 Algorithm details ‣ 3 Algorithm ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.
772:<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We claim that if <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rankzn(A)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>rank</mi><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rankzn(A)=2</annotation></semantics></math>, then the two lattice points <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{a},\boldsymbol{b}\in K_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒃</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{a},\boldsymbol{b}\in K_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> that generate the points corresponding to the columns of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> must satisfy <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{a}\in K_{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{a}\in K_{-}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}\in K_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}\in K_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>. To see this, notice that we need to generate <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{u},\boldsymbol{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒗</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{u},\boldsymbol{v}</annotation></semantics></math> which themselves generate the cone <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{u},\boldsymbol{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒗</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{u},\boldsymbol{v}</annotation></semantics></math> are both nonnegative integer combinations of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{a},\boldsymbol{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒃</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{a},\boldsymbol{b}</annotation></semantics></math> then, in particular, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{u},\boldsymbol{v}\in\cone(\boldsymbol{a},\boldsymbol{b})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒗</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>cone</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒃</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{u},\boldsymbol{v}\in\cone(\boldsymbol{a},\boldsymbol{b})</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\subset\cone(\boldsymbol{a},\boldsymbol{b})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>cone</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒃</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\subset\cone(\boldsymbol{a},\boldsymbol{b})</annotation></semantics></math> and without loss of generality, we have <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{a}\in K_{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{a}\in K_{-}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}\in K_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}\in K_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, as claimed.</p>
788:<math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{u}=k\boldsymbol{a}+\ell\boldsymbol{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒂</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒃</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{u}=k\boldsymbol{a}+\ell\boldsymbol{b}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k,\ell\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k,\ell\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we get that <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\boldsymbol{a}=\boldsymbol{u}-\ell\boldsymbol{b}\in K_{-}\cap(\boldsymbol{u}-K_{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒂</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒃</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\boldsymbol{a}=\boldsymbol{u}-\ell\boldsymbol{b}\in K_{-}\cap(\boldsymbol{u}-K_{+})</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence for each integer point in the triangle <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=K_{-}\cap(\boldsymbol{u}-K_{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=K_{-}\cap(\boldsymbol{u}-K_{+})</annotation></semantics></math>, we get a possible <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{a}</annotation></semantics></math> by choosing the minimal lattice point on the ray through that point (see Figure <a href="#S3.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 3.1.3 Bounding the Search Space ‣ 3.1 Algorithm details ‣ 3 Algorithm ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>).</p>
803:<p id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This procedure generates a list of candidates <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\boldsymbol{a},\boldsymbol{b})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒃</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\boldsymbol{a},\boldsymbol{b})</annotation></semantics></math> for generators. For each we must check if they indeed generate all the points <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{c}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒄</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{c}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to the columns of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>. If any of them do, we have found that the nonnegative integer rank of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is two, if none of them do, the nonnegative integer rank of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> must be higher.</p>
821:<p id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If we do find generators <math id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{a}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒃</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}</annotation></semantics></math> we then can optionally proceed to reconstruct the matrix factorization. Note that the map that sends <math id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\col(A)\cap\mathbb{Z}_{+}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>col</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\col(A)\cap\mathbb{Z}_{+}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{+}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{+}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> has an inverse that can be written as <math id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(x)=Bx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(x)=Bx</annotation></semantics></math> where the columns of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> are simply the preimages of the vectors <math id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,0)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
824:<p id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We claim that <math id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=(B\begin{bmatrix}\boldsymbol{a}&amp;\boldsymbol{b}\end{bmatrix})\begin{bmatrix}\boldsymbol{w}_{1}&amp;\cdots&amp;\boldsymbol{w}_{m}\end{bmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>𝒂</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>𝒃</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=(B\begin{bmatrix}\boldsymbol{a}&amp;\boldsymbol{b}\end{bmatrix})\begin{bmatrix}\boldsymbol{w}_{1}&amp;\cdots&amp;\boldsymbol{w}_{m}\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math> provides a nonnegative integer rank <math id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> factorization of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>. To see this just note that</p>
865:The resulting cones are pictured in Figure <a href="#S3.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ Example 3.1. ‣ 3.1.4 Factorization from generators ‣ 3.1 Algorithm details ‣ 3 Algorithm ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. Since they are already in canonical form, we have <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=I_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=I_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, the identity matrix.</p>
884:<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">so that <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rankzn(A)\neq 2." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>rank</mi><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rankzn(A)\neq 2.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
886:<figure id="S3.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.05957v2/NNI_rank2_alg.png" id="S3.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:238/200;" width="238" height="200" alt="Refer to caption">
887:<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>Cones of Example <a href="#S3.Thmthm1" title="Example 3.1. ‣ 3.1.4 Factorization from generators ‣ 3.1 Algorithm details ‣ 3 Algorithm ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>: <math id="S3.F8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> in blue, <math id="S3.F8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> in green, and <math id="S3.F8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{-}</annotation></semantics></math> is just the <math id="S3.F8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>-axis.</figcaption>
891:<p id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The complexity of this algorithm is theoretically not much better than full exhaustive search, as the step of searching for candidate factors in the triangle <math id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is polynomial in <math id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|u\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|u\|</annotation></semantics></math> which in turn is polynomial in <math id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|A\|_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|A\|_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> and the size of the matrix. Running the code for the Example <a href="#S2.Thmthm4" title="Example 2.4. ‣ 2.1 A geometric reformulation ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a> with growing <math id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> we see that in fact the time taken seems to grow faster than linearly with the size of the entries (see <a href="#S3.F9" title="In 3.2 Numerical Testing ‣ 3 Algorithm ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>).</p>
896:<p id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This algorithm cannot easily be extended to ranks higher than two. The main reason for this is that both the cone <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> and the cone <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> get much more complicated in higher dimensions, and the containment between them is highly nontrivial. If the rank was <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, our analogous candidate factors would be integer points <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{a}_{1},...,\boldsymbol{a}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{a}_{1},...,\boldsymbol{a}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> that span a simplex that contains <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, but there is no simple way of searching over these in the same way the cone decomposition allows us in rank <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
905:<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To demonstrate the effectiveness of our algorithm, we generate a collection of rank 2 nonnegative integer matrices to factor. We compare the average time to factor the full matrix versus the average time to reduce to a <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 3</annotation></semantics></math> representative as described in Section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Reduction to the 3×3 case ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> for various matrix sizes. We also record the percentage of test matrices that are found to have nonnegative integer rank 2. All algorithms are implemented in SageMath <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> and run on a laptop (2020 MacBook Air, M1 chip). The runtimes recorded in the following tests are meant to be indicative of general trends and as a proof of concept of the algorithm; they should not be taken as benchmarks and may vary with implementation, which we have not attempted to optimize. The code is available by request from the second author.</p>
921:in SageMath that returns lattice vectors <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> proportionally to <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\exp(-|x|^{2}/(2\sigma^{2}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em">/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp(-|x|^{2}/(2\sigma^{2}))</annotation></semantics></math> for a chosen lattice and parameter <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. In our case, the lattice is <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and we vary <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, which has the effect of increasing the size of the entries of our matrix as we increase it. If we want an <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times m</annotation></semantics></math> matrix, we first generate <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> nonzero lattice vectors in the nonnegative orthant to be columns of a matrix <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we generate additional nonzero lattice vectors and add them as rows of a matrix <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> if they live in the dual of <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cone(\cols(C))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>cone</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>cols</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cone(\cols(C))</annotation></semantics></math> until <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> rows. The resulting product <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=BC" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=BC</annotation></semantics></math> will be a nonnegative integer matrix of (regular) rank 2.</p>
924:<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.2 Numerical Testing ‣ 3 Algorithm ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows the results of numerical testing on 1000 matrices with <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\{3,5,10,50\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\{3,5,10,50\}</annotation></semantics></math> and 100 matrices with <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=100</annotation></semantics></math> with each of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in\{3,6,10,25\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in\{3,6,10,25\}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each choice of parameters, we list the average largest entry over the set of matrices, the average number of seconds to factorize each matrix, and the number of matrices that have nonnegative integer rank 2.</p>
927:<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">From the tables, we can see that there is a general correlation between the size of the largest entry and the factorization time. Similarly, there seems to be some correlation between <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and factorization time; take, for example, <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=3,\sigma=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=3,\sigma=10</annotation></semantics></math> versus <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=50,\sigma=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=50,\sigma=6</annotation></semantics></math> which have roughly the same average largest entry, but <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=3</annotation></semantics></math> is around 10 times faster on average.</p>
930:<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Testing Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Acknowledgments ‣ 4 Conclusion ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> on random <math id="S3.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times n</annotation></semantics></math> rank 2 matrices.</figcaption>
937:<td id="S3.T1.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">NNI rank 2</td></tr>
1110:<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Direct factorization vs. reduce then factor on <math id="S3.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times n</annotation></semantics></math> matrices.</figcaption>
1117:<th id="S3.T2.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">NNI rank 2</th></tr>
1122:<th id="S3.T2.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r">factor</th>
1124:<th id="S3.T2.2.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r">factor</th>
1172:<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 3.2 Numerical Testing ‣ 3 Algorithm ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we record the average factorization times for 100 randomly-generated <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10\times 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\times 10</annotation></semantics></math> matrices, as well as for 3 randomly generated <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1000\times 1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1000</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1000\times 1000</annotation></semantics></math> matrices. We also record the average time to reduce each matrix in the set to the <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 3</annotation></semantics></math> case, as described in Section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Reduction to the 3×3 case ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>, and the resulting average time to factor each <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 3</annotation></semantics></math>. Although there is some improvement in the factorization time from the <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10\times 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\times 10</annotation></semantics></math> case to its reduction, it is negligible in comparison to the time it takes to perform the reduction. However, at <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1000</annotation></semantics></math>, we see that first performing the reduction significantly improves the overall factorization time.</p>
1175:<p id="S3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">For our second test set, we further investigate the relationship between the size of the matrix entries and the factorization time. Additionally, to control for the fact that when a matrix has nonnegative integer rank 2, factorization could be fast if we happen to find appropriate generators early, we attempt to construct matrices that are likely to have higher rank. To do this we take inspiration from Example <a href="#S2.Thmthm4" title="Example 2.4. ‣ 2.1 A geometric reformulation ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>. In Figure <a href="#S3.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ 3.2 Numerical Testing ‣ 3 Algorithm ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, we see the effect of increasing <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the average entry size, on the factorization time for the <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 3</annotation></semantics></math> matrix <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, which has nonnegative integer rank 3.</p>
1177:<figure id="S3.F9" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.05957v2/NNI_rank2_Ex2.4_Factor_times.jpg" id="S3.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:333/218;" width="333" height="218" alt="Refer to caption">
1178:<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span>Factoring times for matrices of Example <a href="#S2.Thmthm4" title="Example 2.4. ‣ 2.1 A geometric reformulation ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a> for increasing <math id="S3.F9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
1181:<p id="S3.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We generate a similar set of 1000 <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 3</annotation></semantics></math> matrices that have entries close to some positive integer <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, which we choose from a uniform distribution on <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[3,100]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>3,100</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[3,100]</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, we use the same lattice distribution sampler as before with <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=2</annotation></semantics></math> centered at <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t,t)^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t,t)^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> to generate 3 points inside <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cone((1,0)^{\top},(1,2)^{\top})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>cone</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cone((1,0)^{\top},(1,2)^{\top})</annotation></semantics></math> that form a diagram similar to that of Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Example 2.6. ‣ 2.1 A geometric reformulation ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. To obtain the corresponding matrix we evaluate the 3 points at <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y,</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2x-y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2x-y</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
1184:<p id="S3.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">In Figure <a href="#S3.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ 3.2 Numerical Testing ‣ 3 Algorithm ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, we plot the average value <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> of the entries of each matrix versus its factorization time. Red stars indicate matrices of nonnegative integer rank 3, while blue circles indicate rank 2.</p>
1190:<p id="S3.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">While we see a slight upward trend in these figures, it is largely among outliers. The average factorization time among all matrices (of which 514 were rank 2) was 0.00812 seconds. The majority of matrices are simply factored too quickly to identify any meaningful trend.</p>
1198:<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We interpret the nonnegative integer factorization problem geometrically, as finding a particular set of generators for points in a rational cone. In this way we are able to introduce an algorithm that can efficiently answer <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Problem 1.2 (The rank2 problem). ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Problem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>.
1199:Even from our preliminary implementation of Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Acknowledgments ‣ 4 Conclusion ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we can see that it is possible to check if even large dense matrices have nonnegative integer rank 2.
1203:<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, as we have seen, the current approach has serious limitations. The algorithm fundamentally relies on the fact that we are only considering matrices of (regular) rank 2. While much of our interpretation generalizes to higher ranks, many open questions remain regarding how to apply this geometric insight to exactly compute, or even simply bound, the nonnegative integer rank beyond the rank 2 case.</p>
1214:<figure id="algorithm1" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
1215:<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="algorithm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span>NNI Rank 2 Factorization Algorithm</figcaption>
1216:<div id="algorithm1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
1217:<div id="algorithm1.4.1" class="ltx_listingline">
1219:<span id="algorithm1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span id="algorithm1.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> </span>Integer matrix <math id="algorithm1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="A=[a_{1}\cdots a_{n}]\in\mathbb{Z}^{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=[a_{1}\cdots a_{n}]\in\mathbb{Z}^{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>; index of canonization <math id="algorithm1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>.
1221:<div id="algorithm1.4.2" class="ltx_listingline">Initialize factors <math id="algorithm1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}=[\,],F_{2}=[\,]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">[</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">[</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}=[\,],F_{2}=[\,]</annotation></semantics></math>
