/tmp/paper2602.TH19sM/main_edits.tex:728:While we see a slight upward trend in these figures, it is largely among outliers. The average factorization time among all matrices (of which 514 were rank 2) was 0.00812 seconds. The majority of matrices are simply factored too quickly to identify any meaningful trend. 
/tmp/paper2602.TH19sM/main_edits.tex:732:We interpret the nonnegative integer factorization problem geometrically, as finding a particular set of generators for  points in a rational cone. In this way we are able to introduce an algorithm that can efficiently answer \Cref{Q:rank2}.  
/tmp/paper2602.TH19sM/main_edits.tex:733:Even from our preliminary implementation of Algorithm~\ref{alg:NNI2}, we can see that it is possible to check if even large dense matrices have nonnegative integer rank~2. 
/tmp/paper2602.TH19sM/main_edits.tex:734:In the case of these large matrices, we can take advantage of our reduction to the equivalent $3\times 3$ problem to further improve the computational time. 
/tmp/paper2602.TH19sM/main_edits.tex:736:However, as we have seen, the current approach has serious limitations. The algorithm fundamentally relies on the fact that we are only considering matrices of (regular) rank 2. While much of our interpretation generalizes to higher ranks, many open questions remain regarding how to apply this geometric insight to exactly compute, or even simply bound, the nonnegative integer rank beyond the rank~2 case. 
