422:<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Observation 2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
510:<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.8</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
518:<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">To simplify the argument, note that by <a href="#S2.Thmthm5" title="Observation 2.5. ‣ 2.1 A geometric reformulation ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Observation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a> we can assume that <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in the nonnegative quadrant and one of its rays is generated by <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,0)</annotation></semantics></math>.
527:<p id="S2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We already knew how to go from the rank<math id="S2.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> problem for an <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times m</annotation></semantics></math> matrix <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> to Problem <a href="#S2.Thmthm2" title="Problem 2.2. ‣ 2.1 A geometric reformulation ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>, with <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> integer points. Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm8" title="Proposition 2.8. ‣ 2.2 Reduction to the 3×3 case ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a> now tells us that we can go from that problem to a the rank<math id="S2.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> problem on a <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times m</annotation></semantics></math> matrix. We want to interpret this reduction directly in terms of matrices, bypassing the geometric construction. The general underlying idea is the following.</p>
564:<p id="S2.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The argument in Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm8" title="Proposition 2.8. ‣ 2.2 Reduction to the 3×3 case ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a> boils down to saying that for every <math id="S2.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times m</annotation></semantics></math> nonnegative integer matrix <math id="S2.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> we can construct a <math id="S2.SS2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times m</annotation></semantics></math> nonnegative integer matrix <math id="S2.SS2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the conditions of Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm9" title="Lemma 2.9. ‣ 2.2 Reduction to the 3×3 case ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.9</span></a>. In what follows we show how to construct such a matrix explicitly.</p>
582:<p id="S2.SS2.p15.1" class="ltx_p">We can now leverage this construction, applying it twice. If we have an <math id="S2.SS2.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times m</annotation></semantics></math> matrix of rank <math id="S2.SS2.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> fow which we want to solve the minrank problem, we can apply Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm8" title="Proposition 2.8. ‣ 2.2 Reduction to the 3×3 case ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a> to obtain a <math id="S2.SS2.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times n</annotation></semantics></math> matrix with the same solution. Now, since transposing a matrix does not change its ranks, we can apply the proposition again to the transpose of this matrix, attaining a <math id="S2.SS2.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 3</annotation></semantics></math> matrix that will also have the same solution as the original one. We thus obtain the following fact.</p>
585:<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.10</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
736:<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This approach certainly works, but is impractical due to the size of the search space. The main idea of our proposed algorithm is to use the geometric interpretation of <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 A geometric reformulation ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> to restrict the search space for nonnegative integer vectors that factorize the given matrix (see Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Acknowledgments ‣ 4 Conclusion ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). The intuition for reducing the search space can be seen in Example <a href="#S2.Thmthm4" title="Example 2.4. ‣ 2.1 A geometric reformulation ‣ 2 A geometric view of the rank2 problem ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>, where we note that in order to generate the points of <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\col(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>col</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\col(A)</annotation></semantics></math> nonnegatively, our 2 generators must somehow live “outside” this set of points. We formalize this idea below.</p>
930:<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Testing Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Acknowledgments ‣ 4 Conclusion ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> on random <math id="S3.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times n</annotation></semantics></math> rank 2 matrices.</figcaption>
1190:<p id="S3.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">While we see a slight upward trend in these figures, it is largely among outliers. The average factorization time among all matrices (of which 514 were rank 2) was 0.00812 seconds. The majority of matrices are simply factored too quickly to identify any meaningful trend.</p>
1199:Even from our preliminary implementation of Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Acknowledgments ‣ 4 Conclusion ‣ Matrices of nonnegative integer rank two" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we can see that it is possible to check if even large dense matrices have nonnegative integer rank 2.

