417:\section{Algorithm}
420:In the previous section we saw that checking if an $n \times m$ matrix of rank $2$ also has nonnegative integer rank $2$ can be reduced to checking it for a $3 \times 3$ matrix. Unfortunately, this does not get us any closer to solving this problem, it just tells us that the $3 \times 3$ version of the problem is as hard as the original one. In this section, we present an algorithm that actually solves the rank2 problem. 
424:This approach certainly works, but is impractical due to the size of the search space. The main idea of our proposed algorithm is to use the geometric interpretation of \Cref{subsec:geometric} to restrict the search space for nonnegative integer vectors that factorize the given matrix (see Algorithm~\ref{alg:NNI2}). The intuition for reducing the search space can be seen in Example~\ref{ex:graphicexample2}, where we note that in order to generate the points of $\col(A)$ nonnegatively, our 2 generators must somehow live ``outside'' this set of points. We formalize this idea below. 
