% To search for $b_3$, note that the minors of the matrices $[b_1,b_2], [b_1,b_3]$, and $[b_3,b_2]$ are all integers, and each minor of $[b_1,b_3]$ and $[b_3,b_2]$ is obtained from the corresponding minor of $B = [b_1,b_2]$ by multiplying by $\beta$ and $\alpha$, respectively. Thus, if $d$ is the greatest common divisors of the minors of $B$, then both $d\alpha$ and $d\beta$ must be integers. 
% %one just have to note that if we take $d$ to be the greatest common divisor between all the $2 \times 2$ minors of the matrix $B$, then $d\alpha$ and $d \beta$ must be integer. Just note that the minors of $[b1,b3]$ and $[b2,b3]$ are attained just by multiplying the minors of $B$ by $\beta$ and $\alpha$ respectively. 
